{"instance_id": "rich_4077", "observer_elapsed_seconds": 35.008669314000144, "kind": "agent_event", "agent": "repair_agent", "agent_run_id": "agent_run_1", "parent_agent_run_id": null, "event_id": "708c6275-37af-46fa-a21a-f479b883fc1a", "invocation_id": "e-58d3015b-7f5a-4272-87f6-4ef3bb36354d", "branch": null, "partial": false, "calls": [{"tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-aa5765568fe2d561", "function_name": "edit_file", "arguments": {"allow_multiple": false, "filepath": "rich/file_proxy.py", "new_string": "    def isatty(self) -> bool:\n        return getattr(self.__file, \"isatty\", lambda: False)()\n\n    def __getattr__(self, name: str) -> Any:\n        return getattr(self.__file, name)", "old_string": "    def __getattr__(self, name: str) -> Any:\n        return getattr(self.__file, name)"}}], "responses": [], "visible_text": "The issue `proxy isatty` suggests that when `FileProxy` wraps a file object, subsequent calls to `isatty()` on that proxy or the wrapped file might fail or behave incorrectly, especially when the proxy is used in contexts that expect standard file methods.\n\nIn `rich/file_proxy.py`, the `FileProxy` class inherits from `io.TextIOBase` and delegates most attribute access to `self.__file` via `__getattr__`.\n\n```python\n    def __getattr__(self, name: str) -> Any:\n        return getattr(self.__file, name)\n```\n\nIf `self.__file` is a standard file object, calling `isatty()` on `FileProxy` will call `isatty()` on `self.__file`. If `self.__file` is a proxy or a stream that doesn't implement `isatty()`, or if the context expects `FileProxy` itself to handle this, it can lead to issues.\n\nThe fix is to explicitly implement `isatty()` in `FileProxy` to delegate the check to the underlying file object, or perhaps provide a default behavior if the underlying object doesn't support it, ensuring that the proxy itself behaves like a standard file object regarding this method.\n\nSince `FileProxy` is designed to wrap a file, it should ideally pass through calls like `isatty()`.\n\nI will add an `isatty` method to `FileProxy` that delegates the call to `self.__file`.\n\nFile: `rich/file_proxy.py`\n\n```python\n    def __getattr__(self, name: str) -> Any:\n        return getattr(self.__file, name)\n```\n\nShould become:\n\n```python\n    def __getattr__(self, name: str) -> Any:\n        if name == \"isatty\":\n            return getattr(self.__file, \"isatty\")\n        return getattr(self.__file, name)\n```\nHowever, since `__getattr__` is called when an attribute is not found, if `isatty` is not present on `self.__file`, it will raise an `AttributeError`. If I just add `isatty` to `FileProxy`, it will be found before `__getattr__` is called.\n\nLet's check the existing implementation of `__getattr__` again. It returns `getattr(self.__file, name)`. If `self.__file` has `isatty`, it will be returned. If it doesn't, it raises `AttributeError`.\n\nThe issue might be that `isatty` is expected to be available on the proxy itself, even if the underlying file doesn't support it, or that the way it's being called is problematic.\n\nIf I add `isatty` explicitly to `FileProxy`, it will be available.\n\n```python\n    def isatty(self) -> bool:\n        return getattr(self.__file, \"isatty\", lambda: False)()\n```\nThis seems like a robust way to implement it, providing a default of `False` if `isatty` is not available on the wrapped file.\n\nLet's apply this change to `rich/file_proxy.py`.\n\n```python\n    def __getattr__(self, name: str) -> Any:\n        return getattr(self.__file, name)\n```\nI will replace this with the implementation that includes `isatty`.\n\nWait, if I add `isatty` to the class body, it will be found before `__getattr__`.\n\n```python\nclass FileProxy(io.TextIOBase):\n    # ... init and properties ...\n\n    def isatty(self) -> bool:\n        return getattr(self.__file, \"isatty\", lambda: False)()\n\n    def __getattr__(self, name: str) -> Any:\n        return getattr(self.__file, name)\n```\nThis looks correct and minimal.\n\nI will use `edit_file`.<channel|>", "error_code": null, "error_message": null}
